FAQ = [
 ("Is a hard or soft tonneau cover better?",
  "Neither is better for everyone. A hard cover (tri-fold, quad-fold, retractable or one-piece) locks under a locked tailgate, can carry a published load, and resists a knife. A soft cover (roll-up or soft tri-fold) costs less, weighs less, goes on in minutes and gives back more of the bed. If tools or gear stay in the bed overnight, buy hard. If you mainly want to keep rain, road dirt and casual eyes out, and you haul tall loads often, a soft cover does the job for a fraction of the price."),
 ("Are hard tonneau covers waterproof?",
  "No cover is truly waterproof, hard or soft. Makers describe seals, drain rails and drain tubes, which is the language of water resistance. The weak spots are the tailgate gap, the front bulkhead seal and, on retractables, the canister, which is why Retrax ships drain tubes that route through the bed's corner holes. Owners of well-installed hard folds do report dry beds, but treat the bed as a damp space and keep electronics, paper and anything that mildews in sealed boxes."),
 ("How much weight can a tonneau cover hold?",
  "Only hard covers publish a number, and it is an evenly distributed rating. On the covers in the site's F-150 and Silverado guides, the Gator EFX is rated 300 lb, the BAKFlip MX4 and UnderCover Ultra Flex 400 lb, and the RetraxPRO MX 500 lb. Evenly distributed means spread flat across the panels, like snow or a sheet of plywood. A knee, a boot heel or a toolbox corner concentrates that load on a small area and can still dent a panel. Soft covers carry no rating."),
 ("Can someone cut through a soft tonneau cover?",
  "Yes. Vinyl, even the 24 oz marine-grade material used on covers like the Tyger T3, stops weather and people looking in, but a knife goes through it in seconds. The frame clamps under the rail, so a soft cover is only as secure as the vinyl. That is fine for groceries, luggage on a road trip or gear you would not miss. For tools, bikes or anything expensive left overnight, a hard cover that locks under a locked tailgate is the minimum."),
 ("Do tonneau covers improve gas mileage?",
  "Maybe a little, and not reliably. Jalopnik's roundup points to a Forbes writer's RAM TRX, which went from about 12 mpg to 12.5–13 mpg on the highway with a cover and showed little difference in town, and it notes that owners' results are hit and miss. That is one truck and one driver, not a controlled test. Buy a cover for security, weather and looks; count any fuel saving as a small bonus, and don't expect a heavy cover to pay for itself at the pump."),
 ("Which tonneau cover gives full bed access?",
  "Soft roll-ups roll tight to the cab and give back the whole bed. Retractables open the full length but the canister takes space at the front; on the F-150 guide's RetraxPRO MX that is about 10–11 in. Standard hard and soft tri-folds leave roughly the front third covered by the folded stack unless you unclamp and lift the cover out. Some hard folds, such as the UnderCover Ultra Flex, stand the folded panels up against the cab for full access."),
 ("Will a tonneau cover work with a bed rack or toolbox?",
  "Only if both parts are designed for it. A standard folding cover leaves nothing to bolt a rack to, and crossbox toolboxes sit where the cover's rails clamp. Railed retractables such as the RetraxPRO XR add T-slot rails along the bed sides for crossbars or a bed rack. Some hard folds are sold in toolbox versions that end short of the box. Factory in-bed storage boxes, like GM's side boxes, are excluded by every cover in the site's Silverado guide."),
 ("How long does it take to install a tonneau cover?",
  "Most clamp-on covers need no drilling. Maker figures in the site's guides run from 5–10 minutes for the Tyger T3 soft tri-fold, to under 30 minutes for hard folds like the BAKFlip MX4 and Gator EFX, to under an hour for a TruXedo roll-up or a RetraxPRO retractable. Retractables and roll-ups take longer because the rails and tensioners need adjusting and drain tubes need routing. One-piece hinged lids are heavy and usually need a second person."),
 ("Do soft tonneau covers sag in snow?",
  "They can. A soft cover is a tensioned sheet of vinyl over crossbows, so wet snow collects on it and pushes it down between the bows. Clear it rather than leaving it to sit, and check the tension after cold snaps. Roll-ups with automatic tensioners, such as the TruXedo Lo Pro, keep the vinyl tighter as the temperature swings. If snow sits on the truck for days at a time, a hard cover with a published distributed rating is the safer choice."),
 ("What about fiberglass one-piece covers?",
  "One-piece hinged lids, often fiberglass painted to match the truck, give the cleanest look and seal well because there are no panel joints. They lift on struts and lock with a key or the tailgate. The trade-offs are bed access, weight and flexibility: the lid opens as one piece, it limits how tall a load you can carry, and many need two people to take off. Paint-matched lids also need the right paint code ordered, which makes them harder to move to a different truck."),
]

